    "result_text": "The suite is green. There's only one test class in the project (`AcceptanceTest`), and it passes along with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` field so it returns `true` when the owner's city already holds between 40 and 49 owners (approaching the capacity limit of 50), while leaving the hard rejection at 50 unchanged.\n\nChanges, following the existing `bulkSignupWarning` pattern:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property to the owner schema (drives the generated `OwnerDto`).\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column field with getter/setter.\n- **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column. The test profile uses hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column had to exist in the schema.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"capacityWarning\", ignore = true)` on the `OwnerFieldsDto \u2192 Owner` mapping (derived, not client-supplied).\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added a `CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40` constant and a `capacityWarning(city)` helper that returns `true` when the city's pre-persist owner count is in `[40, 50)`, counted case-insensitively/trimmed exactly as `rejectCityAtCapacity` does. It's set on the owner during `addOwner`, right after the existing `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\nThe existing hard rejection at 50 (`rejectCityAtCapacity`) is untouched.",
